LUASYNTH(1)                 User Commands                 LUASYNTH(1)

NAME
    luasynth - polyphonic Lua synthesizer

SYNOPSIS
    luasynth [patch.lsynth]

DESCRIPTION
    Lua Synthesizer is a separate instrument from the C synth program.
    It plays up to eight voices with velocity, sustain, two oscillators
    per voice, ADSR envelopes, a shared resonant stereo low-pass filter,
    vibrato, stereo spread, and a ping-pong delay. Output is stereo S16
    PCM at 48000 Hz through audiod. Excess notes steal a quiet released
    voice first, then a quiet held voice (oldest when equally loud).

    Oscillators offer sine, saw, pulse and triangle. Saw and pulse use
    polynomial edge correction to reduce aliasing. Oscillator B has
    separate detune and octave controls. Blend and pulse width change
    the timbre. The amplitude envelope can also open the shared filter.
    Vibrato depth is in cents and its rate is shown in Hz. Delay time,
    wet mix and feedback are independent. Saturation bounds the output.

    Choose Warm pad, Bright keys, Deep bass, Glass, Pulse lead or Pure
    sine from Preset. Demo cycles through chords to audition a sound.
    Save patch and Load patch preserve all sound controls in a plain
    key=value .lsynth file. Loading a patch does not execute Lua code.
    Invalid files leave the current patch unchanged.

KEYBOARD
    Click the on-screen keyboard to give it keyboard focus.
    Z S X D C V G B H N J M     lower chromatic octave
    Q 2 W 3 E R 5 T 6 Y 7 U I   upper octave and the next C
    Space                       sustain while held
    [ and ]                     shift the keyboard octave
    Esc                         panic: stop voices, demo and delay
    Ctrl+Q or window Close      close the application

    Several keys can be held at once. Click and drag on the on-screen
    keyboard to play a glissando. Keyboard and mouse holds on the same
    note are tracked independently. Losing window focus releases held
    notes. Moving focus to other controls releases keyboard notes.

AUDIO
    Desktop sessions start audiod. A console can start it with audiod &
    before starting a desktop application. The status line reports active
    voices, the output peak and audio underruns. A dedicated native thread
    owns synthesis and audio playback, while the main thread handles the
    window and controls. Display updates do not block audio generation.
    Synthesis is interpreted Lua, so many voices and a slow virtual CPU
    can still cause underruns. Closing the window stops and joins the
    audio worker before releasing the application.

FILES
    /home/.local/share/apps/luasynth/engine.lua    synthesis and patch format
    /home/.local/share/apps/luasynth/ui.lua        window and controls
    /home/.local/share/apps/luasynth/worker.lua    dedicated audio loop
    /home/.local/share/apps/luasynth/controller.lua command and meter exchange
    /home/luasynth.lsynth                         default patch save location

